{% extends "base.html" %}
{% block content %}
<div class="docs-page grid grid-cols-[240px_1fr] min-h-[600px]">

  {# ===== SIDEBAR NAVIGATION ===== #}
  <aside class="py-8 px-6 border-r"
         style="border-color: var(--border); background: var(--bg-card-soft); position: sticky; top: 0; height: fit-content;">
    <div class="gc-section-label mb-4">Documentation</div>
    <nav class="space-y-1">
      <a href="#about" class="docs-nav-link">1 · About</a>
      <a href="#usage" class="docs-nav-link">2 · How to use</a>
      <div class="pl-4 space-y-0.5 mt-1 mb-2">
        <a href="#usage-step1" class="docs-nav-sublink">Step 1 — Upload</a>
        <a href="#usage-step2" class="docs-nav-sublink">Step 2 — Method</a>
        <a href="#usage-step3" class="docs-nav-sublink">Step 3 — Curate</a>
        <a href="#usage-step4" class="docs-nav-sublink">Step 4 — Results</a>
      </div>
      <a href="#methods" class="docs-nav-link">3 · Methods</a>
      <a href="#data" class="docs-nav-link">4 · Data requirements</a>
      <a href="#case-study" class="docs-nav-link">5 · Case study</a>
      <a href="#contact" class="docs-nav-link">Contact</a>
    </nav>
  </aside>

  {# ===== CONTENT ===== #}
  <div class="py-10 px-12 max-w-[820px]" style="line-height: 1.7;">

    {# Page title #}
    <div class="mb-10">
      <div style="font-size: 11px; letter-spacing: 0.08em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--text-muted); font-weight: 500;">
        Documentation
      </div>
      <h1 class="text-[28px] mt-1 mb-2" style="font-weight: 500; letter-spacing: -0.02em; color: var(--text-strong);">
        GeneCurator: Human-in-the-Loop Gene Selector
      </h1>
      <p class="text-[13.5px]" style="color: var(--text-muted);">
        A reference guide covering the tool's purpose, workflow, and methods.
      </p>
    </div>

    {# ==================== ABOUT ==================== #}
    <section id="about" class="mb-12 scroll-mt-6">
      <h2 class="docs-h2">1 · About</h2>
      <p class="docs-p">
        GeneCurator is a web-based analytical framework for gene panel
        selection from differential expression data, developed to bridge
        the gap between fully automated feature selection and manual
        expert curation. The tool implements a wizard-based workflow in
        which unsupervised and supervised algorithms first propose a
        candidate gene set, and the domain expert then refines that
        selection through evidence-informed inclusion or exclusion of
        individual genes.
      </p>
      <p class="docs-p">
        The motivation for this design stems from a well-documented
        limitation of purely algorithmic feature selection in
        transcriptomic studies: statistical ranking, while reproducible,
        does not integrate the biological context that shapes a
        defensible gene panel. Expression signatures dominated by highly
        variable but functionally redundant transcripts (e.g.,
        immunoglobulin variable regions in tumor-infiltrating B-cell
        contexts) can obscure the smaller set of biologically actionable
        targets. Conversely, purely manual curation is time-consuming,
        inconsistent between reviewers, and difficult to reproduce.
        GeneCurator's human-in-the-loop paradigm preserves the
        reproducibility of algorithmic ranking while exposing the
        ranking to structured human oversight at each decision point.
      </p>
      <p class="docs-p">
        The application is intended for exploratory analysis of
        differential expression outputs from bulk RNA-seq, microarray,
        or pseudo-bulk single-cell RNA-seq experiments. It is not a
        diagnostic instrument and its outputs should not be used to
        guide clinical decision-making.
      </p>
    </section>

    {# ==================== HOW TO USE ==================== #}
    <section id="usage" class="mb-12 scroll-mt-6">
      <h2 class="docs-h2">2 · How to use</h2>

      <div id="usage-step1" class="mb-8 scroll-mt-6">
        <h3 class="docs-h3">Step 1 — Upload your data</h3>
        <p class="docs-p">
          Prepare a differential expression table in CSV, TSV, or plain
          text format. The table must contain at minimum three columns:
          a gene identifier (HGNC symbol or Ensembl ID), a p-value, and
          a log2 fold change. Additional columns (adjusted p-values,
          base mean expression, etc.) are preserved but not required.
        </p>
        <p class="docs-p">
          Drop the file into the upload panel or use the file browser.
          The tool auto-detects the delimiter and column types. After
          loading, confirm the column mapping — the three dropdowns let
          you specify which columns hold the gene symbol, p-value, and
          log2 fold change. To try the workflow without your own data,
          use "Load toy dataset" to begin with a small immune-axis
          example.
        </p>
        <p class="docs-p">
          Once the file is loaded and the columns are mapped, press
          <em>Continue</em>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div id="usage-step2" class="mb-8 scroll-mt-6">
        <h3 class="docs-h3">Step 2 — Choose a feature selection method</h3>
        <p class="docs-p">
          Seven algorithms are available. The choice depends on your
          statistical assumptions and downstream goal:
        </p>
        <ul class="docs-ul">
          <li><strong>Traditional DEG (Volcano)</strong> — hard thresholds on p-value and |log2FC|. Best when you have well-calibrated statistics and a specific biological effect size in mind.</li>
          <li><strong>Random Forest (Ensemble)</strong> — tree-based importance scoring. Robust to noise, handles non-linear relationships, no distributional assumptions.</li>
          <li><strong>Gradient Boosting</strong> — XGBoost-style boosted trees. Slightly higher variance than Random Forest but often better on subtle signals.</li>
          <li><strong>SHAP Ranking</strong> — Shapley value attribution over a boosted-tree model. Gives per-gene importance decomposed by contribution to the classifier.</li>
          <li><strong>LASSO</strong> — L1-regularized regression. Yields sparse, interpretable coefficient-based selection.</li>
          <li><strong>Mutual Information</strong> — non-parametric dependence measure between each gene and the direction label.</li>
          <li><strong>Correlation Ranking</strong> — Spearman rank correlation with direction. Simple, fast baseline.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="docs-p">
          Set the number of top genes to select (default 50) or the
          p-value and log2FC thresholds if you chose Traditional DEG.
          Then press <em>Run feature selection</em>. Depending on the
          dataset size, execution takes between a few seconds and a
          couple of minutes.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div id="usage-step3" class="mb-8 scroll-mt-6">
        <h3 class="docs-h3">Step 3 — Curate the algorithm's picks</h3>
        <p class="docs-p">
          The algorithm's selected genes appear in a ranked table on the
          left. Click a row to load its details in the Gene Inspector on
          the right. The inspector shows the gene's log2FC, p-value,
          importance score, and — if the UniProt proteome table has
          been loaded — protein function summary and known disease
          associations.
        </p>
        <p class="docs-p">
          Untick the checkbox next to a gene to remove it from the final
          panel. Re-tick to restore. To add a gene that the algorithm
          did not pick (e.g., a gene the literature strongly supports
          for your biological question), use the Gene Inspector dropdown
          to select the symbol and press <em>Add to panel</em>.
        </p>
        <p class="docs-p">
          The counts strip at the top tracks how far your curated panel
          has diverged from the algorithm's original picks. Press <em>View
          results</em> when the panel is complete.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div id="usage-step4" class="scroll-mt-6">
        <h3 class="docs-h3">Step 4 — Interpret the results</h3>
        <p class="docs-p">Four analysis tabs are available:</p>
        <ul class="docs-ul">
          <li><strong>Discovery summary</strong> — volcano plot of the full dataset with curated panel genes highlighted, plus a table of the top-ranked discoveries.</li>
          <li><strong>PPI network</strong> — protein-protein interaction graph among the curated genes, built from the STRING database. Isolated nodes indicate genes without catalogued interactions in the panel context.</li>
          <li><strong>Enrichment</strong> — functional over-representation analysis of the panel against fifteen gene set libraries organized into five categories: pathways, ontology, diseases, cell types, and drugs. For KEGG results, click "map" next to any term to view the official KEGG pathway diagram with your genes colored by log2FC direction.</li>
          <li><strong>Log2FC profile</strong> — magnitude distribution of the curated panel, useful for detecting one-directional signatures (e.g., predominantly down-regulated panels).</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="docs-p">
          Export options are provided at the bottom of the results page:
          an Excel workbook containing all analysis results and metadata,
          or a plain CSV of the final panel for use in downstream tools.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    {# ==================== METHODS ==================== #}
    <section id="methods" class="mb-12 scroll-mt-6">
      <h2 class="docs-h2">3 · Methods</h2>

      <div class="mb-6">
        <h3 class="docs-h3">Feature selection</h3>
        <p class="docs-p">
          Supervised algorithms (Random Forest, Gradient Boosting, SHAP,
          LASSO) treat gene expression direction (up- versus
          down-regulated, or significant versus non-significant) as the
          classification label and rank genes by their contribution to
          the fitted model. Unsupervised approaches (Mutual Information,
          Correlation Ranking) measure statistical dependence between
          each gene and the direction label without fitting a model.
          Traditional DEG applies hard thresholds directly. All
          implementations use scikit-learn 1.4+ (Random Forest, LASSO,
          Mutual Information), XGBoost 2.0+ (Gradient Boosting), and the
          SHAP 0.44+ TreeExplainer for Shapley value computation.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="mb-6">
        <h3 class="docs-h3">Functional enrichment</h3>
        <p class="docs-p">
          Over-representation analysis is performed against fifteen
          Enrichr-derived gene set libraries: KEGG 2026, Reactome 2024,
          WikiPathways 2024, BioPlanet, MSigDB Hallmark 2020, GO
          Biological Process / Molecular Function / Cellular Component
          2026, DisGeNET, Disease Perturbations from GEO (up and down),
          PanglaoDB Augmented 2021, DSigDB, and Drug Perturbations from
          GEO (up and down). For each term, a one-sided Fisher's exact
          test is applied, treating the union of genes annotated in that
          library as the background universe. P-values are corrected for
          multiple testing using the Benjamini-Hochberg procedure across
          all terms within a library.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="mb-6">
        <h3 class="docs-h3">Protein-protein interaction network</h3>
        <p class="docs-p">
          The PPI subgraph is constructed by querying the STRING database
          for interactions restricted to the curated panel genes,
          retaining only edges with a combined confidence score above
          0.4. The graph is rendered with a force-directed layout; nodes
          without qualifying interactions are displayed as isolates.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="mb-6">
        <h3 class="docs-h3">KEGG pathway visualization</h3>
        <p class="docs-p">
          A local snapshot of the KEGG PATHWAY database (approximately
          360 human pathway diagrams) was retrieved via the KEGG REST
          API. When a KEGG term is expanded, the corresponding KGML file
          is parsed to identify gene entries; the associated pathway PNG
          is rendered with a semi-transparent colored overlay on each
          gene box, red for up-regulated and blue for down-regulated,
          with opacity scaled by the log2 fold change magnitude. The
          snapshot date is displayed within the Enrichment tab.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h3 class="docs-h3">Gene evidence integration</h3>
        <p class="docs-p">
          The Gene Inspector cross-references the reviewed panel against
          a locally stored subset of the UniProt human reference proteome
          (UniProtKB, release 2026_03). Retrieved fields include the
          protein name, sequence length, canonical function annotation,
          and disease involvement records. Absence of a UniProt record
          is reported explicitly rather than silently suppressed.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    {# ==================== DATA REQUIREMENTS ==================== #}
    <section id="data" class="mb-12 scroll-mt-6">
      <h2 class="docs-h2">4 · Data requirements</h2>

      <div class="mb-6">
        <h3 class="docs-h3">Input format</h3>
        <p class="docs-p">
          Accepted file extensions are .csv, .tsv, and .txt with a
          single header row. The delimiter is auto-detected. Files up to
          50 MB are supported; larger files should be pre-filtered
          (e.g., restricted to expressed genes with mean count above a
          low threshold) before upload.
        </p>
        <p class="docs-p">
          The three required columns can carry any name and appear in
          any position; column mapping is performed after upload.
          Numeric columns must use a period as the decimal separator.
          Missing values in the p-value or log2FC columns cause the
          corresponding rows to be dropped during preprocessing.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="mb-6">
        <h3 class="docs-h3">Species and identifiers</h3>
        <p class="docs-p">
          All bundled gene set libraries and the UniProt reference
          proteome are human. Non-human datasets can be uploaded and
          analyzed algorithmically, but the enrichment, PPI, and Gene
          Inspector components will not produce biologically meaningful
          results. Gene identifiers should be provided as HGNC symbols
          for optimal cross-referencing; Ensembl IDs are accepted but
          may reduce the overlap with the Enrichr libraries, which are
          distributed with symbol-based annotations.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h3 class="docs-h3">Recommended dataset characteristics</h3>
        <p class="docs-p">
          The tool has been validated on differential expression tables
          containing between 5,000 and 20,000 genes. Panels of 20-100
          genes are typically produced by the default algorithm settings;
          panels below 15 genes may yield underpowered enrichment
          results, while panels above 200 genes tend to produce diffuse,
          low-specificity enrichment signals.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    {# ==================== CASE STUDY ==================== #}
    <section id="case-study" class="mb-12 scroll-mt-6">
      <h2 class="docs-h2">5 · Case study: TCGA-BRCA</h2>
      <p class="docs-p">
        The bundled case study uses The Cancer Genome Atlas breast
        invasive carcinoma cohort (TCGA-BRCA) to illustrate the failure
        mode that motivated the tool's development: purely
        statistics-driven feature selection can produce panels that are
        technically correct but biologically incomplete. The dataset
        contains a tumor-versus-normal differential expression table
        with approximately 15,000 genes, derived from ~400 primary
        tumors and ~113 solid tissue normals using the DESeq2 workflow
        on STAR-Counts. The R script used to regenerate the table is
        distributed with the source code
        (<code>data/prepare_brca_deg.R</code>).
      </p>

      <div class="mb-6">
        <h3 class="docs-h3">The algorithm-only panel</h3>
        <p class="docs-p">
          Running Random Forest with the default top-50 setting produces
          a panel dominated by two signatures. The first is proliferation
          machinery — cell cycle regulators, mitotic spindle components,
          DNA replication factors — including BIRC5, TOP2A, UBE2C, PLK1,
          RRM2, AURKA/B, CDK1, CDC20, CENPF, KIF20A, and CCNB1/B2. These
          are among the strongest-effect genes in almost every cancer
          DEG table and reliably dominate any automated ranking. The
          second signature comes from the loss of normal breast tissue
          composition — milk proteins (CSN2, LALBA, CSN1S1, WAP) and
          adipocyte markers (ADIPOQ, FABP4, PLIN1, LEP, CIDEA) — all
          strongly down-regulated in the tumor cohort because tumor
          tissue is not lactating adipose tissue.
        </p>
        <p class="docs-p">
          This panel is statistically defensible. Every gene has a
          Benjamini-Hochberg adjusted p-value well below 10<sup>−30</sup>.
          A reviewer reading the enrichment results would see clear
          hallmark signatures for G2/M Checkpoint, E2F Targets, and
          Mitotic Spindle — the standard "this tumor is proliferating"
          answer that applies to every solid cancer.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="mb-6">
        <h3 class="docs-h3">What the automated panel is missing</h3>
        <p class="docs-p">
          The canonical breast cancer biomarkers that inform every
          clinical decision — ESR1 (estrogen receptor), PGR (progesterone
          receptor), ERBB2 (HER2), FOXA1, GATA3, and BCL2 — are all
          significantly differentially expressed with reasonable effect
          sizes (log2 fold change 1.2 to 1.8, adjusted p-value below
          10<sup>−7</sup>). However, they are not among the top 50 genes
          selected by Random Forest because their absolute effect sizes
          are dwarfed by the proliferation and stroma signals. In the
          bundled case-study table they rank between positions 370
          (ERBB2) and 610 (ESR1) — comfortably outside the automated
          panel.
        </p>
        <p class="docs-p">
          The clinical consequence of this omission is substantial.
          These six genes underpin the commercial breast cancer
          molecular assays (Oncotype DX, MammaPrint, PAM50), define
          therapeutic decisions (endocrine therapy for ESR1-positive
          disease, trastuzumab for ERBB2-amplified disease), and provide
          the biological framework within which any breast cancer
          hypothesis must be interpreted. A panel of 50 breast tumor
          genes that omits these markers would be considered
          incomplete by any oncologist, regardless of the statistical
          rigor of the selection procedure.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="mb-6">
        <h3 class="docs-h3">Expert curation</h3>
        <p class="docs-p">
          Using the Expert Review interface, a domain expert removes
          approximately fifteen genes from the algorithmic panel that
          contribute redundant or biologically diffuse signal — several
          immunoglobulin transcripts, the strongest adipocyte markers
          (which reflect tissue composition rather than tumor biology),
          and cell-cycle members whose function is already represented
          by another selected gene. The expert then adds the six
          canonical breast cancer markers (ESR1, PGR, ERBB2, FOXA1,
          GATA3, BCL2), the tumor suppressor PTEN, the transcription
          factor TP53, and the PI3K/AKT pathway member PIK3CA.
        </p>
        <p class="docs-p">
          The resulting curated panel is smaller in nominal statistical
          strength — several added genes have larger p-values than the
          removed genes — but is substantially more coherent
          biologically. The panel now represents four distinct axes of
          breast cancer biology (hormone signaling, HER2 pathway,
          proliferation, and tumor suppressor loss) rather than one
          dominant axis (proliferation) with adjacent tissue changes.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h3 class="docs-h3">Downstream comparison</h3>
        <p class="docs-p">
          Functional enrichment against the KEGG 2026 library shifts
          from generic proliferation and cancer terms (Cell Cycle,
          p53 Signaling, Pathways in Cancer) to a clearly
          tissue-specific profile that now includes Breast Cancer
          (hsa05224) and Estrogen Signaling (hsa04915) with substantially
          improved false discovery rates. Enrichment against MSigDB
          Hallmark loses none of the proliferation signal (G2/M
          Checkpoint, E2F Targets remain enriched) but gains estrogen
          response signatures (Estrogen Response Early and Late) that
          were absent from the algorithmic panel. The PPI network
          becomes measurably more connected, with the hormone receptor
          axis forming a dense subgraph around ESR1 that is absent from
          the algorithm-only version. The KEGG pathway map for Breast
          Cancer (hsa05224), which was inactive in the algorithmic
          panel, now displays 8 of 12 signature nodes colored by
          log2FC direction.
        </p>
        <p class="docs-p">
          The comparison demonstrates the two-part argument for
          human-in-the-loop feature selection made in this work.
          Automated feature selection is a valuable filter — it
          identified 47 of the 50 proliferation-associated genes that
          the expert retained in the final panel — but it is not
          sufficient on its own. Expert curation restores biologically
          essential axes that pure statistics cannot see, and the
          combination is materially better than either component alone.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    {# ==================== CONTACT ==================== #}
    <section id="contact" class="mb-6 scroll-mt-6">
      <h2 class="docs-h2">Contact</h2>
      <p class="docs-p">
        <strong>Evgenia Papadaki</strong><br>
        Bioinformatics and Human Electrophysiology Laboratory (BiHELab)<br>
        Department of Informatics, Ionian University, Corfu, Greece<br>
        <a href="mailto:epapadaki@ionio.gr" style="color: var(--brand-500);">epapadaki@ionio.gr</a>
      </p>
    </section>

  </div>
</div>

